The bug is the end index in `slice`. `slice(start, end)` excludes `end`, so `arr.length - 1` drops the last element.

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n, arr.length);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

Or, omitting the end argument, which defaults to the array length:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(-n);
}
```

Be careful with `n = 0`. `arr.slice(-0)` is `arr.slice(0)`, which returns the whole array, not an empty one. The first version handles that case correctly, since `arr.slice(arr.length, arr.length)` returns `[]`. If `n` can be 0, use the first version or add a guard like `if (n <= 0) return [];`.

